        conceptEn: "From TypeScript 4.5, tail-recursive conditional types do not consume depth so quickly: type Trim<S> = S extends ` ${infer R}` ? Trim<R> : S can strip many spaces. “Tail” means the recursive call is the result of the branch, with no wrapper such as Trim<R> | Extra. If you write [Trim<R>, ...] or G<Trim<R>>, the optimisation will not apply. For long strings and tuples prefer the tail form; for more complex transforms, split a step into an intermediate helper.",
